== what a real `git push --force` sends (local transport, same send-pack code path) ==
$ git --version
git version 2.43.0
$ GIT_TRACE_PACKET=1 git push --force ../up.git HEAD:refs/heads/main   # orphan commit, non-fast-forward
packet: receive-pack> ec3572bcc841c17b559cf593d1320dd01e24d8d0 refs/heads/main\0report-status report-status-v2 delete-refs side-band-64k quiet atomic ofs-delta object-format=sha1 agent=git/2.43.0
packet:         push> ec3572bcc841c17b559cf593d1320dd01e24d8d0 dc39a4ff07963511c4367a2467db9675e0db38ee refs/heads/main\0 report-status-v2 side-band-64k quiet object-format=sha1 agent=git/2.43.0
packet: receive-pack> ok refs/heads/main

The client capability list is `report-status-v2 side-band-64k quiet object-format=sha1
agent=git/2.43.0`. Neither `force-ref-updates` nor `push-force` appears, and neither is
a capability git sends at all — `force-ref-updates` is a JGit/Gerrit server-side
capability. Router 0.109.0 decides `forced` from exactly those two strings
(src/git_proxy.rs `body_requests_force`), so `refuse_destructive_updates` never sees
`forced = true` from a real git client and a history rewrite is forwarded to GitHub.

Measured end-to-end in git-transport.log section 5: the router forwarded
`+ f5e3e3e...a861cb3 (forced update)` to an unrelated orphan commit. The branch was
restored from the host immediately afterwards.
